<file path="docs/COLLAB_TESTING.md">
  1: # Collections, Workspaces & Team — Testing Guide
  2: 
  3: > Status: API-level E2E green — `node apps/backend/e2e_collab.cjs` → **55/55**
  4: > checks (self-cleaning: creates unique users/workspaces/collections, deletes
  5: > them in teardown). This file covers the browser walkthrough + what the E2E
  6: > asserts so you know what is covered.
  7: >
  8: > Shared dev/prod DB: the E2E is re-runnable and never touches demo users
  9: > (admin/researcher/pro.researcher) or global counts.
 10: 
 11: ## Quick runs
 12: 
 13: ```bash
 14: # Full API-level suite for this module (self-cleaning, ~1 min)
 15: node apps/backend/e2e_collab.cjs
 16: 
 17: # Regression cross-check after any change (papers + billing modules)
 18: node apps/backend/e2e_papers.cjs
 19: node apps/backend/e2e_admin.cjs
 20: ```
 21: 
 22: ## What e2e_collab.cjs covers
 23: 
 24: 1. **Registration security** — registering with `role: "ADMIN"` is clamped to
 25:    RESEARCHER (client-supplied roles rejected; paid roles come from billing
 26:    webhooks, elevated roles from admin team management).
 27: 2. **Collections** — create (owned workspace required), foreign read 403,
 28:    invite (PENDING visible in received list), accept, re-invite of ACCEPTED →
 29:    400, EDIT member update allowed, EDIT member delete → 403 (owner-only),
 30:    outsider update → 403, search scoping (owner finds own private collection,
 31:    non-members never see it), delete → 404 after.
 32: 3. **Workspaces** — create, invite by email, received list (route-order fixed),
 33:    accept, EDITOR can't rename/add members, member list, owner promotes to
 34:    MANAGER, MANAGER can invite but cannot invite as OWNER (escalation guard),
 35:    sent-invites list.
 36: 4. **Team** — TEAM_LEAD promotion by admin, team member list scoped to shared
 37:    workspaces (shared member + self visible; non-shared owner/outsider NEVER
 38:    leak), cannot modify non-shared users (403), cannot grant ADMIN (403),
 39:    cannot change own role (400), researchers blocked from team routes (403).
 40: 5. **Analytics** — workspace analytics: member of workspace reads it; foreign
 41:    workspace → 403 (IDOR closed).
 42: 6. **Notifications** — invitee receives in-app INVITE notifications.
 43: 7. **Teardown** — deletes every created workspace + soft-deletes every created
 44:    user; verified by querying the shared DB afterwards (0 active e2e users).
 45: 
 46: ---
 47: 
 48: ## Manual browser walkthrough
 49: 
 50: Prep: `yarn dev:backend` + `yarn dev:frontend`; use admin@scholarflow.com and
 51: two throwaway accounts (register via the UI — they start as RESEARCHER;
 52: promote one via Admin → Users or the team page as admin).
 53: 
 54: ### 1. Workspaces (`/dashboard/workspaces`)
 55: - [ ] Create workspace (modal + standalone create page) → lands on detail.
 56: - [ ] Tabs: overview / collections / papers / members / activity / settings.
 57: - [ ] Invite dialog: email + role; **OWNER option works only for the owner**
 58:       (MANAGER gets an honest 403 toast, not a silent failure).
 59: - [ ] Invitee sees the invite on `/dashboard/workspaces/shared` → accept →
 60:       member with the invited role (VIEWER/EDITOR/MANAGER).
 61: - [ ] EDITOR cannot rename/delete/add members (buttons may be hidden — API
 62:       403s regardless); MANAGER can invite + manage non-owner members.
 63: - [ ] Owner-only: delete workspace (type-to-confirm) — collection under the
 64:       workspace disappears too (cascade soft-delete).
 65: - [ ] Settings tab: color + toggles persist; activity feed shows workspace
 66:       events (create/update/delete/member ops now write ActivityLogEntry, so
 67:       they appear on the Team activity page too).
 68: 
 69: ### 2. Collections (`/dashboard/collections`)
 70: - [ ] Create (2-step wizard) requires an owned workspace (403 otherwise).
 71: - [ ] Detail: invite by email → invitee accepts from
 72:       `/dashboard/collections/shared` (PENDING → ACCEPTED).
 73: - [ ] Re-inviting an accepted member → error toast (not a silent reset).
 74: - [ ] EDIT member can edit + manage papers, **cannot delete** (owner-only).
 75: - [ ] Delete (trash icon) → confirm → toast + navigate back to the list.
 76: - [ ] Invites sent/received tabs show statuses incl. Expired
 77:       (invitations auto-expire after INVITE_EXPIRY_DAYS, default 7d).
 78: 
 79: ### 3. Team (`/dashboard/team`) — TEAM_LEAD+ only
 80: - [ ] RESEARCHER hitting `/dashboard/team*` → redirected (client guard) —
 81:       no 403 storm.
 82: - [ ] Members list shows ONLY users sharing a workspace with you (never the
 83:       whole platform).
 84: - [ ] Role change: you can't grant ADMIN; you can't modify someone who
 85:       doesn't share a workspace with you; you can't change your own role.
 86: - [ ] Invitations: sent/received with accept/decline now actually working —
 87:       Accept on a received invitation joins the workspace (check Shared
 88:       Workspaces afterwards); Resend/Cancel work on sent ones.
 89: - [ ] Settings: save toggles; Danger Zone now explains account deletion
 90:       (no dead Archive/Delete buttons).
 91: 
 92: ### 4. In-app notifications
 93: - [ ] Bell badge appears when someone invites you to a workspace/collection
 94:       and when they accept your invite (SSE, live without refresh).
 95: 
 96: ## Known notes
 97: - Invite expiry: `INVITE_EXPIRY_DAYS` env (default 7); the hourly
 98:   `invitationSweeper` flips stale PENDING → EXPIRED; accept/decline also
 99:   reject past-expiry invites with 400 regardless of sweep timing.
100: - Team invitations are workspace invitations (no Team entity exists); the
101:   team invite email links to `/dashboard/workspaces/shared` where acceptance
102:   actually happens.
103: - CollectionPaper status/star is collection-wide (not per-user) by decision.
104: - Socket rooms: paper/discussion/workspace joins are membership-checked
105:   (owner/workspace-member/thread-scope); standalone socket-server enforces the
106:   room-name allowlist only (no DB there).
</file>

<file path="docs/DATABASE.md">
  1: # Database Setup Guide
  2: 
  3: This guide covers setting up PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension, configuring Prisma, and working with database migrations.
  4: 
  5: By the end of this guide, you'll have a running PostgreSQL database connected to Scholar-Flow with all tables created.
  6: 
  7: ## Prerequisites
  8: 
  9: - **PostgreSQL 15+** installed (check with `psql --version`)
 10: - **pgvector extension** available (required for semantic search features)
 11: - **Git** — for cloning the repo (already done if you followed the Setup Guide)
 12: 
 13: ## Step 1: Install PostgreSQL
 14: 
 15: ### Option A: Ubuntu/Debian (WSL or Linux)
 16: 
 17: ```bash
 18: sudo apt install -y postgresql-18 postgresql-18-pgvector
 19: sudo pg_ctlcluster 18 main start
 20: ```
 21: 
 22: ### Option B: macOS (Homebrew)
 23: 
 24: ```bash
 25: brew install postgresql@18
 26: brew install pgvector
 27: brew services start postgresql@18
 28: ```
 29: 
 30: ### Option C: Docker (Works on All Platforms)
 31: 
 32: ```bash
 33: docker run -d \
 34:   --name scholar-postgres \
 35:   -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=admin \
 36:   -e POSTGRES_DB=scholarflow_dev \
 37:   -p 5432:5432 \
 38:   pgvector/pgvector:pg18
 39: ```
 40: 
 41: This runs PostgreSQL 18 with pgvector pre-installed. The database `scholarflow_dev` is created automatically.
 42: 
 43: ### Option D: Windows (Direct)
 44: 
 45: Download from [postgresql.org](https://www.postgresql.org/download/windows/) and install the EDB installer, selecting the pgvector component during installation.
 46: 
 47: ## Step 2: Create the Database
 48: 
 49: Once PostgreSQL is running, create the database:
 50: 
 51: ```bash
 52: # Using createdb (simplest)
 53: createdb scholarflow_dev
 54: 
 55: # Or using psql
 56: sudo -u postgres psql -c "CREATE DATABASE scholarflow_dev;"
 57: ```
 58: 
 59: ### Set a Password (if needed)
 60: 
 61: ```bash
 62: sudo -u postgres psql -c "ALTER USER postgres PASSWORD 'admin';"
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: ## Step 3: Enable the pgvector Extension
 66: 
 67: ```bash
 68: sudo -u postgres psql -d scholarflow_dev -c "CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;"
 69: sudo -u postgres psql -d scholarflow_dev -c "CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;"
 70: ```
 71: 
 72: **What these do:**
 73: - `vector` — enables AI-powered semantic search (stores embeddings)
 74: - `pg_trgm` — enables trigram text search (for fuzzy matching)
 75: 
 76: ### Verify pgvector is Enabled
 77: 
 78: ```bash
 79: sudo -u postgres psql -d scholarflow_dev -c "SELECT * FROM pg_extension WHERE extname = 'vector';"
 80: ```
 81: 
 82: You should see a row with `extname = 'vector'`.
 83: 
 84: ## Step 4: Configure Prisma Connection
 85: 
 86: In `apps/backend/.env`, set these two variables:
 87: 
 88: ```env
 89: DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:admin@localhost:5432/scholarflow_dev
 90: DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:admin@localhost:5432/scholarflow_dev
 91: ```
 92: 
 93: Replace `postgres:admin` with your PostgreSQL username and password.
 94: 
 95: **Why two URLs?**
 96: - `DATABASE_URL` — used by Prisma Accelerate (cloud proxy) for runtime queries
 97: - `DIRECT_DATABASE_URL` — used by migrations and the pg adapter for direct connections
 98: 
 99: In local development, both can point to the same local database.
100: 
101: ## Step 5: Run Migrations
102: 
103: ```bash
104: yarn db:migrate
105: ```
106: 
107: Run this from: the repo root
108: 
109: This creates all database tables defined in `apps/backend/prisma/schema.prisma`.
110: 
111: **First time:** Prisma will prompt for a migration name. Enter something descriptive like:
112: ```
113: initial_setup_with_pgvector
114: ```
115: 
116: **Expected output:**
117: ```
118: Your database is now in sync with your schema.
119: ```
120: 
121: ### Migration Discipline Checklist
122: 
123: - [ ] Always use `yarn db:migrate` — never `prisma db push` (db push bypasses migration history)
124: - [ ] Run `yarn db:generate --sql` after every migration to rebuild the typed client
125: - [ ] Keep migrations additive (don't drop columns that might have data in production)
126: - [ ] Never drop pgvector columns or vector indexes in production
127: - [ ] If you see drift, create a reconciliation migration — don't reset
128: 
129: ## Step 6: Generate the Prisma Client
130: 
131: ```bash
132: yarn db:generate
133: ```
134: 
135: Run this from: the repo root
136: 
137: This builds the Prisma client with typed SQL query support. Run this any time the schema changes.
138: 
139: ## Step 7: (Optional) Seed Sample Data
140: 
141: ```bash
142: yarn db:seed
143: ```
144: 
145: This populates the database with demo users and sample data for development.
146: 
147: ## Step 8: Verify with Prisma Studio
148: 
149: ```bash
150: yarn db:studio
151: ```
152: 
153: This opens a browser-based GUI at http://localhost:5555 where you can browse and edit database tables visually. It's a great way to inspect data during development.
154: 
155: ## Common Database Workflows
156: 
157: ### After Editing the Prisma Schema
158: 
159: ```bash
160: # 1. Create migration and apply it
161: yarn db:migrate
162: 
163: # 2. Regenerate the client
164: yarn db:generate
165: ```
166: 
167: ### Reset Local Database (Drops ALL Data)
168: 
169: ```bash
170: yarn db:reset
171: ```
172: 
173: This runs `prisma migrate reset --force` — it drops all tables and re-runs all migrations. Use this when you want a clean slate. You'll need to re-seed after:
174: 
175: ```bash
176: yarn db:seed
177: ```
178: 
179: ### View Database Manually
180: 
181: ```bash
182: psql -d scholarflow_dev -c "\dt"           # List all tables
183: psql -d scholarflow_dev -c "\d Paper"       # Describe a specific table
184: ```
185: 
186: ### Check for Migration Drift
187: 
188: If the production database has changed outside of migrations (e.g., someone ran `prisma db push`), you may see drift. Detect it:
189: 
190: ```bash
191: psql "postgres://postgres:admin@localhost:5432/scholarflow_dev" -At -c \
192:   "SELECT column_name FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name = 'Paper' AND table_schema = 'public' ORDER BY ordinal_position;"
193: ```
194: 
195: Fix drift by creating a reconciliation migration:
196: 
197: ```bash
198: yarn db:migrate --create-only --name reconcile_schema_drift
199: ```
200: 
201: ## Prisma Client Usage Tips
202: 
203: ### Prefer Prisma ORM (typed queries)
204: 
205: ```typescript
206: const papers = await prisma.paper.findMany({
207:   where: { workspaceId },
208:   select: { id: true, title: true, createdAt: true },
209: })
210: ```
211: 
212: ### Use Raw SQL Only When Needed
213: 
214: Raw SQL is required for pgvector operations and complex aggregations. Use `$queryRaw` with template literals:
215: 
216: ```typescript
217: const results = await prisma.$queryRaw<PaperChunk[]>`
218:   SELECT id, content, page, idx
219:   FROM "PaperChunk"
220:   ORDER BY embedding <=> ${embedding}::vector
221:   LIMIT 10
222: `
223: ```
224: 
225: ### Select Only What You Need
226: 
227: Always use explicit `select` in list queries. Never fetch `embedding` or binary file content in list views.
228: 
229: ## Troubleshooting
230: 
231: **Problem: `FATAL: role "postgres" does not exist`**
232: Cause: PostgreSQL was installed but no default user was created.
233: Fix: `sudo -u postgres createuser --superuser $USER` or use the OS user.
234: 
235: **Problem: `Extension "vector" not available`**
236: Cause: The pgvector extension isn't installed.
237: Fix: Install the `postgresql-18-pgvector` package (see Step 1), or use the Docker image with pgvector pre-installed.
238: 
239: **Problem: `Port 5432 already in use`**
240: Cause: Another PostgreSQL instance is running, or the port is occupied.
241: Fix: Check with `sudo lsof -i :5432` and stop the other process, or change the port in `.env`.
242: 
243: **Problem: `Error: getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND`**
244: Cause: PostgreSQL isn't running or the connection URL has a typo.
245: Fix: Start PostgreSQL (`sudo pg_ctlcluster 18 main start`) and double-check `DATABASE_URL`.
246: 
247: **Problem: Prisma migration fails with "no changes detected"**
248: Cause: Prisma doesn't see a difference between the schema and the database.
249: Fix: This is normal if you already applied the migration. If you need to force a new migration, make a real change to `schema.prisma` first.
250: 
251: ## Related Docs
252: 
253: - [Setup Guide](./SETUP.md) — full environment setup
254: - [Environment Variables](./ENVIRONMENT.md) — env var reference
255: - [Redis Setup](./REDIS_SETUP.md) — optional Redis for background jobs
</file>

<file path="docs/DEVELOPMENT.md">
  1: # Scholar-Flow Development Guide
  2: 
  3: This guide covers day-to-day development workflows: how to add features, fix bugs, run quality checks, and work with the monorepo.
  4: 
  5: ## Prerequisites
  6: 
  7: You should have completed the [Setup Guide](./SETUP.md) first. By now you should have:
  8: 
  9: - A running PostgreSQL database
 10: - Three dev servers running on ports 3000, 5000, and 5001
 11: - Access to http://localhost:3000 in your browser
 12: 
 13: ## Project Structure (Where Things Live)
 14: 
 15: ```
 16: apps/
 17: ├── frontend/src/
 18: │   ├── app/              # Next.js App Router pages
 19: │   ├── components/       # Reusable UI components
 20: │   │   ├── auth/         # Login, register, etc.
 21: │   │   ├── layout/       # Navigation, sidebar, header
 22: │   │   ├── ui/           # shadcn/ui primitives
 23: │   │   └── customUI/     # Project-specific components
 24: │   ├── lib/              # Utilities, auth config, API clients
 25: │   └── redux/            # RTK Query slices and store
 26: ├── backend/src/
 27: │   └── app/
 28: │       ├── modules/      # Feature modules (Papers, Auth, Billing, etc.)
 29: │       │   └── [Module]/
 30: │       │       ├── [name].controller.ts
 31: │       │       ├── [name].service.ts
 32: │       │       ├── [name].routes.ts
 33: │       │       └── [name].validation.ts
 34: │       ├── middleware/    # Auth, rate limiting, error handling
 35: │       ├── routes/        # Route registration
 36: │       └── shared/        # Prisma singleton, email service, etc.
 37: ├── socket-server/
 38: │   └── src/
 39: │       └── server.ts     # Socket.io server entry point
 40: ```
 41: 
 42: ## Common Development Tasks
 43: 
 44: ### Adding a new page (frontend)
 45: 
 46: ```bash
 47: # Create the page file
 48: mkdir -p apps/frontend/src/app/papers/new
 49: touch apps/frontend/src/app/papers/new/page.tsx
 50: ```
 51: 
 52: Pages use the App Router. If the page needs interactive state, add `"use client"` at the top. Otherwise it's a Server Component by default (preferred).
 53: 
 54: ```typescript
 55: // apps/frontend/src/app/papers/new/page.tsx
 56: import { NewPaperForm } from "@/components/papers/NewPaperForm"
 57: 
 58: export default function NewPaperPage() {
 59:   return <NewPaperForm />
 60: }
 61: ```
 62: 
 63: ### Adding a new API endpoint (backend)
 64: 
 65: Each feature has its own module directory under `apps/backend/src/app/modules/`:
 66: 
 67: ```bash
 68: # Create module files
 69: touch apps/backend/src/app/modules/Reports/reports.controller.ts
 70: touch apps/backend/src/app/modules/Reports/reports.service.ts
 71: touch apps/backend/src/app/modules/Reports/reports.routes.ts
 72: touch apps/backend/src/app/modules/Reports/reports.validation.ts
 73: ```
 74: 
 75: Then register the routes in `apps/backend/src/app/routes/index.ts`.
 76: 
 77: **Structure rules:**
 78: - Controllers are thin — all business logic goes in services
 79: - All request/response validation uses Zod schemas
 80: - Use `catchAsync` for async error handling
 81: - Use `ApiError` for known error responses
 82: - Use `sendResponse` / `sendSuccessResponse` for consistent response format
 83: 
 84: ### Adding a new database model
 85: 
 86: 1. Edit `apps/backend/prisma/schema.prisma` to add your model
 87: 2. Run migrations and regenerate the client:
 88: 
 89:    ```bash
 90:    yarn db:migrate        # Creates the migration file and applies it
 91:    yarn db:generate       # Rebuilds the Prisma client with --sql flag
 92:    ```
 93: 
 94: 3. Use the new model in your service:
 95: 
 96:    ```typescript
 97:    import { prisma } from "../../shared/prisma"
 98: 
 99:    const papers = await prisma.paper.findMany({ ... })
100:    ```
101: 
102: **Important:** Always use `yarn db:migrate` (not `prisma db push`) for schema changes. See [Database Guide](./DATABASE.md) for migration discipline.
103: 
104: ### Working with RTK Query (frontend API calls)
105: 
106: All API calls go through RTK Query slices in `apps/frontend/redux/`. Never make raw `fetch` calls inside components.
107: 
108: ```typescript
109: // redux/api/papersApi.ts
110: export const papersApi = createApi({
111:   reducerPath: "papersApi",
112:   baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: "/api" }),
113:   endpoints: (builder) => ({
114:     getPapers: builder.query<Paper[], void>({
115:       query: () => "/papers",
116:     }),
117:   }),
118: })
119: 
120: // In a component:
121: const { data: papers, isLoading } = useGetPapersQuery()
122: ```
123: 
124: ### Adding environment variables
125: 
126: 1. Add the variable to the appropriate `.env.example` file (frontend or backend)
127: 2. Add it to both `.env` (local) and the Vercel dashboard (production)
128: 3. Document it in [docs/ENVIRONMENT.md](./ENVIRONMENT.md)
129: 
130: ## Quality Checks
131: 
132: Run these before committing or opening a PR:
133: 
134: ```bash
135: # TypeScript type checking
136: yarn type-check
137: 
138: # ESLint (catch bugs and style issues)
139: yarn lint
140: 
141: # Run tests
142: yarn test
143: ```
144: 
145: Run all three from the repo root. If any fail, fix the issues before pushing.
146: 
147: ## Database Workflow
148: 
149: ```bash
150: # After editing schema.prisma:
151: yarn db:migrate          # Create and apply migration
152: yarn db:generate         # Rebuild client with typed SQL
153: 
154: # View/edit data visually:
155: yarn db:studio           # Opens Prisma Studio in browser
156: 
157: # Reset dev database (drops all data):
158: yarn db:reset
159: 
160: # Re-seed after reset:
161: yarn db:seed
162: ```
163: 
164: ## Troubleshooting Dev Issues
165: 
166: **Problem: Changes not showing in the browser**
167: Cause: Next.js caches aggressively in dev.
168: Fix: Clear the Next.js cache with `rm -rf apps/frontend/.next` and restart.
169: 
170: **Problem: TypeScript errors after pulling new code**
171: Cause: New Prisma models or API endpoints added by someone else.
172: Fix: Run `yarn db:generate` to rebuild the client, then `yarn type-check`.
173: 
174: **Problem: "Module not found" errors**
175: Cause: A new dependency was added.
176: Fix: Run `yarn install` (or `yarn setup` if the Prisma schema also changed).
177: 
178: **Problem: Port conflict (EADDRINUSE)**
179: Cause: A previous dev server didn't shut down properly.
180: Fix: `lsof -ti:3000 | xargs kill -9` (replace 3000 with the port number).
181: 
182: ## Related Docs
183: 
184: - [Setup Guide](./SETUP.md) — initial environment setup
185: - [Quickstart Guide](./QUICKSTART.md) — get running in 5 minutes
186: - [Contributing Guide](../CONTRIBUTING.md) — how to contribute
187: - [Contributing Guide](../CONTRIBUTING.md) — how to submit PRs
188: - [Database Setup](./DATABASE.md) — PostgreSQL + pgvector + Prisma
189: - [Environment Variables](./ENVIRONMENT.md) — full env var reference
</file>

<file path="docs/ENVIRONMENT.md">
  1: # Scholar-Flow Environment Variables
  2: 
  3: This guide explains every environment variable used by the three apps (frontend, backend, socket-server). Each section tells you what the variable does, where to get its value, and what to set in development.
  4: 
  5: ## Quick Start (Minimum Vars to Get Running)
  6: 
  7: To start the dev servers with basic functionality, you only need to set these:
  8: 
  9: **Backend (`apps/backend/.env`):**
 10: ```env
 11: DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:admin@localhost:5432/scholarflow_dev
 12: DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:admin@localhost:5432/scholarflow_dev
 13: ```
 14: 
 15: **Frontend (`apps/frontend/.env.local`):**
 16: ```env
 17: BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=your-secret-here
 18: NEXTAUTH_SECRET=your-secret-here
 19: ```
 20: 
 21: **Socket-server (`apps/socket-server/.env`):**
 22: ```env
 23: NEXTAUTH_SECRET=same-as-frontend-secret
 24: FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
 25: ```
 26: 
 27: > Copy the example files first: `cp apps/backend/.env.example apps/backend/.env`, and similarly for frontend and socket-server.
 28: 
 29: ## Generating Strong Secrets
 30: 
 31: For any secret (*_SECRET, *_KEY), generate a random 32+ byte value:
 32: 
 33: ```bash
 34: openssl rand -base64 32
 35: # Example output: x7R3kP9mZ2qL8vN4wB6cF1gH5jK0lM2n
 36: ```
 37: 
 38: Or use Node.js:
 39: 
 40: ```bash
 41: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
 42: ```
