  1: # CACHE OPTIMIZATION CONTRACT — FROZEN PREFIX
  2: # This block is always first. Never modify between calls.
  3: # DeepSeek, Kimi, GLM, MiMo: cache matches from byte 0.
  4: # Any edit before byte N invalidates everything after it.
  5: 
  6: ## Prefix stability rules (all models)
  7: - System prompt + tool definitions: STATIC — frozen between calls
  8: - File reads: DYNAMIC — always appended at END of context only
  9: - User message: always last
 10: - Conversation history: append-only, never rewrite earlier turns
 11: - Never inject timestamps, dates, or session-specific values here
 12: 
 13: ## Qwen 3.x cache activation requirement
 14: Qwen models need minimum 1024 tokens to activate prefix caching.
 15: This AGENTS.md + skills content must stay above 1024 tokens total.
 16: Do not shorten this file.
 17: 
 18: ## Agent behavior (cache-preserving)
 19: - Understand the full goal before ANY tool call
 20: - One tool call at a time — no parallel speculation
 21: - Read each file ONCE per session unless you edited it
 22: - Do not re-read files to verify — trust what is in context
 23: - Never duplicate file content across turns — reference by path
 24: - Stop when goal is achieved — do not add unrequested changes
 25: 
 26: ## Multi-Model Cache Optimization
 27: This project supports multiple AI models with prefix caching:
 28: - **DeepSeek V4 Pro / DeepSeek Flash**: Cache hits from byte 0 on matching prefix
 29: - **Kimi K2.6**: Cache hits from byte 0 on matching prefix
 30: - **GLM-4 / MiMo**: Cache hits from byte 0 on matching prefix
 31: - **Qwen 3.x**: Requires minimum 1024 tokens to activate prefix caching
 32: - **Optimization strategy**: Keep AGENTS.md + skills content above 1024 tokens total
 33: - **Rule**: Never inject timestamps, dates, or session-specific values in the frozen prefix
 34: - **Rule**: Never modify the frozen prefix block between calls — any edit before byte N invalidates everything after it
 35: 
 36: ---
 37: 
 38: # Scholar-Flow AI Agent Instructions
 39: 
 40: ## Product
 41: Scholar-Flow is an AI-powered research paper SaaS collaboration platform.
 42: Target users: researchers, students, professors, academic teams.
 43: Version: 1.3.5 — Next.js 16, React 19.2, better-auth, Prisma 7.8.0
 44: 
 45: Core features:
 46: - Research paper upload with AI metadata extraction (title/author/abstract)
 47: - TipTap rich text editor: auto-save, drafts, export PDF/DOCX
 48: - Collections and workspaces with role-based access
 49: - Email sharing with permission management (view/edit)
 50: - Stripe billing: checkout, webhooks, customer portal
 51: - Admin dashboard: real-time CPU/memory/storage/DB metrics (10s refresh)
 52: - pgvector semantic search
 53: - PDF preview (secure iframe)
 54: - AWS S3 for all file storage
 55: 
 56: ---
 57: 
 58: ## Monorepo Structure
 59: Root: Turborepo v2.5.8 + Yarn Berry v4.9.2
 60:   apps/frontend/    → Next.js 16 app (package: @scholar-flow/frontend)
 61:   apps/backend/     → Express.js API (package: @scholar-flow/backend)
 62:   docs/             → Documentation
 63:   .cursor/rules/    → Cursor AI guidelines (read-only)
 64:   figma-make/       → Figma-generated component references
 65: 
 66: ---
 67: 
 68: ## Frontend (apps/frontend)
 69: Framework: Next.js 16 App Router
 70: Language: TypeScript strict mode
 71: State: Redux Toolkit Query (RTK Query) — NOT plain Redux
 72: Auth: better-auth (Google OAuth, GitHub OAuth, email/password) — migrated from NextAuth.js v4
 73: UI: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
 74: Forms: React Hook Form + Zod
 75: Editor: TipTap (tiptap-extension-resizable-image, tiptap-resizable-image)
 76: Virtualization: react-window
 77: Sanitization: sanitize-html
 78: File structure:
 79:   apps/frontend/src/app/         → App Router pages and layouts
 80:   apps/frontend/components/      → Reusable UI components
 81:   apps/frontend/lib/             → Utilities, auth config, API clients
 82:   apps/frontend/redux/           → RTK Query slices and store config
 83:   apps/frontend/proxy.ts         → Auth guards and route protection (replaces middleware.ts)
 84: 
 85: ---
 86: 
 87: ## Backend (apps/backend)
 88: Runtime: Node.js >=22, Express.js, TypeScript
 89: ORM: Prisma 7.8.0 (always use --sql flag: yarn db:generate)
 90: Database: PostgreSQL + pgvector extension
 91: Auth: JWT + bcrypt (refresh tokens, rate limiting)
 92: Storage: AWS S3 (presigned URLs only — never expose credentials)
 93: Payments: Stripe (checkout + webhooks + customer portal)
 94: Validation: Zod schemas
 95: File structure:
 96:   apps/backend/src/app/          → Controllers, routes, middleware
 97:   apps/backend/prisma/           → schema.prisma + migrations
 98:   apps/backend/scripts/          → Seed and utility scripts
 99: 
100: ---
101: 
102: ## Exact Commands (always use Yarn — never npm, never pnpm)
103: yarn dev:turbo           → start both apps via Turborepo
104: yarn dev:frontend        → frontend only
105: yarn dev:backend         → backend only
106: yarn build               → production build (both)
107: yarn lint                → ESLint (both, flat configs)
108: yarn type-check          → TypeScript check (both, needs build first)
109: yarn format              → Prettier
110: yarn clean               → clean all build artifacts
111: yarn db:migrate          → prisma migrate dev
112: yarn db:generate         → prisma generate --sql  ← always --sql
113: yarn db:studio           → Prisma Studio
114: yarn db:seed             → seed database
115: yarn db:reset            → prisma migrate reset --force
116: yarn setup               → install + generate (use for fresh setup)
117: 
118: Note: `yarn test` was removed (test suites deleted by decision — tests are
119: not used; CI runs lint + type-check + build only).
120: 
121: ---
122: 
123: ## Architecture Rules
124: Frontend and backend are fully decoupled — REST API only.
125: Frontend: UI, RTK Query, NextAuth sessions, Redux state.
126: Backend: business logic, Prisma, S3, Stripe, JWT.
127: NEVER import backend code into frontend.
128: NEVER use Prisma in frontend.
129: NEVER bypass the REST API layer.
130: NEVER store files locally in production — use S3.
131: NEVER expose AWS credentials or S3 bucket names in frontend code.
132: 
133: ---
134: 
135: ## Authentication Rules
136: Frontend: NextAuth.js only — Google/GitHub OAuth + email/password.
137: Backend: JWT + bcrypt + refresh tokens.
138: NEVER suggest better-auth or any auth replacement.
139: NEVER bypass auth middleware on protected routes.
140: NEVER store raw passwords — always bcrypt.
141: Session validation: backend verifies JWT, frontend uses NextAuth session.
142: 
143: ### Auth-state persistence (learned the hard way in 1.2.2)
144: 
145: After any auth-state dispatch (`setCredentials` / `clearCredentials`) and BEFORE any `window.location.href` navigation, you MUST `await persistor.flush()`. redux-persist schedules localStorage writes on a microtask; a hard navigation kills that microtask and the next page rehydrates stale state. The 401 cascade and "wrong user shown" bugs in 1.2.1 came from skipping this.
146: 
147: - `apps/frontend/src/lib/authHelpers.ts:signInWithCredentials` and `:completeOAuthSignIn` both call `await flushAuthState()` before returning
148: - `apps/frontend/src/lib/auth/signout.ts` is the canonical 7-step purge — copy that order, do not invent a new flow
149: - OAuth callback pages MUST use `window.location.href` on success, not `router.push` / `router.replace` (soft nav keeps the old tree mounted)
150: 
151: ### better-auth client/server boundary
152: 
153: `authClient` (from `lib/auth/authClient.ts`) transitively imports `better-auth/next-js` and `node:crypto` — both server-only. Never import `authClient` from any client-side auth utility (`signout.ts`, `authHelpers.ts`, etc.). Clear better-auth cookies via `document.cookie` directly. The ChunkLoadError in 1.2.2 came from violating this.
154: 
155: ### RTK Query auth-guard pattern
156: 
157: `shouldFetchUser = Boolean(accessToken && accessToken.length > 0)` — never use `isAuthenticated` alone for guarding queries. Stale persist rehydration can leave `isAuthenticated: true` with `accessToken: null`. The dev-only `console.warn` in `apiSlice.ts:prepareHeaders` surfaces this and must stay.
158: 
159: ### OAuth profile preservation
160: 
161: `createOrUpdateUserWithOAuth` and `createOrUpdateUser` must NEVER overwrite `name` or `image` on update — only fill them in if the existing value is empty. Custom uploads (S3) and renamed profiles belong to the user, not the OAuth provider. `emailVerified` is always refreshed.
162: 
163: ---
164: 
165: ## Stripe Rules (critical — production money flows)
166: Webhooks are the ONLY source of truth for subscription status.
167: NEVER update subscription roles from frontend.
168: ALWAYS verify Stripe webhook signatures before processing.
169: Customer portal accessed via backend endpoint only.
170: Webhook handlers MUST be idempotent (safe to replay).
171: Test mode and live mode keys are different — never mix them.
172: Before touching billing code: read the webhook handler first.
173: Subscription expiry/grace downgrades run in subscriptionSweeper.ts (hourly
174: node-cron, SUB_GRACE_DAYS default 7) — never rely on webhooks alone for
175: downgrades: Stripe keeps failed payments in PAST_DUE dunning for days/weeks.
176: 
177: ## Architecture Learnings (Release 1.3.2 hardening)
178: 
179: ### Canonical dashboard URLs (no role segments)
180: - All dashboard links/redirects MUST be canonical `/dashboard/*` (admin:
181:   `/dashboard/admin`). The legacy `/dashboard/{roleSlug}` routes are REMOVED;
182:   `proxy.ts` keeps only pure back-compat redirects for old bookmarks.
183: - `getDashboardBasePath(role)` in `lib/auth/roles.ts` is the single source of
184:   truth. Do NOT reintroduce `buildRoleScopedPath`/`getRoleSlug`-style helpers.
185: 
186: ### Global search access control
187: - Papers search (raw SQL + count) MUST filter: `uploaderId = user` OR
188:   workspace where user is owner/active member (non-deleted).
189: - People search is ADMIN-ONLY and scoped to the requester's team members
190:   (users sharing a workspace); non-admins silently get empty results —
